From: rive
Subject: [PATCH 0045] trap like posix on Windows under hw bounds, and pass
 crashes in natives on

WAMR's Windows exception handler skipped the faulting instruction, decoded
with Zydis, and let the function run on, so an out of bounds access under
hardware bounds read garbage or wrote nowhere and the call carried on. It
could only decode x86-64, and the interpreter relied on a __try that also
took faults outside wasm from the host's crash reporter.

A call into wasm now goes through os_call_trapping, a small assembly thunk
that keeps the callee saved registers in its own frame, and the callee saved
floating point control, MXCSR and the x87 control word or FPCR, in its
buffer. A trap points the faulting thread at the return of the thunk's call
with that control and resumes it, so the call fails with the trap and
returns to its caller as the posix handler's longjmp makes it. The thunk has
an x64 and an arm64 form, and nothing decodes instructions. Nothing unwinds
through compiled code, which has no unwind info, and the resume address is
on the shadow stack, which CET checks a resumed context against. A longjmp
from the handler fails with STATUS_BAD_FUNCTION_TABLE, and resuming at a
setjmp site fails under CET once a binary carries EH continuation metadata.

The handler is added once for the process, as Windows calls it for every
thread, and removed by its handle. It looks only at access violations, and
at stack overflows when stack checks are hardware ones, whose guard page a
trapped call now resets.

A crash in a native called from wasm never reached a crash reporter that
takes unhandled exceptions, in either bounds mode: the SEH walk stopped at
invokeNative and at compiled code, which have no unwind info, so the thread
start's filter never ran and the process died. invokeNative now has unwind
info, and on x64 each module's code gets a function table whose handler
passes such a crash to the unhandled exception filter itself.

--- a/core/iwasm/aot/aot_loader.c
+++ b/core/iwasm/aot/aot_loader.c
@@ -4511,6 +4511,94 @@ fail:
